per and lower bounds in the non-adaptive case.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe $$S_3$$ -symmetric tridiagonal algebra | Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics | Springer Nature Link (https://doi.org/10.1007/S10801-025-01420-8)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; In the present paper, we introduce a generalization of the tridiagonal algebra called the S_3$S_3$-symmetric tridiagonal algebra. ... For scalars \\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^*$\\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^*$ the S_3$S_3$-symmetric tridiagonal algebra \\mathbb T= \\mathbb T(\\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^* )$\\mathbb T= \\mathbb T(\\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^* )$ is defined by generators\nWe now explain how the tridiagonal algebra appears in the theory of distance-regular graphs. Let \\Gamma $\\Gamma $ denote a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph [30, Definition 11.2]. There are some well-known scalars \\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^*$\\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^*$ that are used to describe the eigenvalue sequence and dual eigenvalue sequence of the Q-polynomial structure [25, Lemma 5.4]. Let X denote the vertex set of \\Gamma $\\Gamma $. The standard module V of \\Gamma $\\Gamma $ is the vector space with basis X. According to [25, Lemma 5.4], for x \\in X$x \\in X$ the vector space V becomes a module for T(\\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^*)$T(\\beta , \\gamma , \\gamma ^*, \\varrho , \\varrho ^*)$ on which A acts as the adjacency map and A^*$A^*$ acts as the dual adjacency map with respect to x [30, Section 11].\n\nLet S_3$S_3$ denote the symmetric group on the set \\lbrace 1,2,3\\rbrace $\\lbrace 1,2,3\\rbrace $. In the present paper, we introduce a generalization of the tridiagonal algebra called the S_3$S_3$-symmetric tridiagonal algebra. This algebra is 